1
00:02:42,580 --> 00:02:48,580
Earth's Final Hours is a science fiction, tv movie film directed by W.D. Hogan.

2
00:05:25,161 --> 00:05:31,161
After dense matter from an imploded white hole hits
Earth, the planet's rotation is devastated.

3
00:08:07,741 --> 00:08:13,741
A group of government agents must locate a lost satellite
network that is the world's only hope for survival.

4
00:10:50,322 --> 00:10:56,322
Lt. Reid, played by Hamza Adam.

5
00:13:32,903 --> 00:13:38,903
Chloe Edwards, played by Julia Benson.

6
00:16:15,483 --> 00:16:21,483
Andy Streich, played by Cameron Bright.

7
00:18:58,064 --> 00:19:04,064
John Streich, played by Robert Knepper.

8
00:21:40,645 --> 00:21:46,645
Arnett, played by Roark Critchlow.

9
00:24:23,225 --> 00:24:29,225
Rothman, played by Bruce Davison.

10
00:27:05,806 --> 00:27:11,806
Tolkien's novels The Hobbit (1937) and The Lord of the Rings (1954–55), set in his fictional world of
Middle-earth, have been the subject of numerous motion picture adaptations across film and television.

11
00:29:48,387 --> 00:29:54,387
Tolkien was skeptical of the prospects of an adaptation.

12
00:32:30,967 --> 00:32:36,967
The rights to adapt his works passed through the hands of several studios, having been briefly leased to Rembrandt Films before being sold
perpetually to United Artists, who then passed them in part to Saul Zaentz who operated the rights under Middle-earth Enterprises.

13
00:35:13,548 --> 00:35:19,548
During this time, filmmakers who attempted to adapt Tolkien's works include William Snyder,
Peter Shaffer, John Boorman, Ralph Bakshi, Peter Jackson, and Guillermo del Toro.

14
00:37:56,129 --> 00:38:02,129
Other filmmakers who were interested in an adaptation included Walt Disney, Al Brodax,
Forrest J Ackerman, Samuel Gelfman, Denis O'Dell, and Heinz Edelmann.

15
00:40:38,709 --> 00:40:44,709
The first commercial adaptation of Tolkien's works was the
Rankin/Bass animated television special The Hobbit (1977).

16
00:43:21,290 --> 00:43:27,290
The first theatrical adaptation was Ralph Bakshi's
animated film The Lord of the Rings (1978).

17
00:46:03,870 --> 00:46:09,870
This was followed by the Rankin/Bass animated
television film The Return of the King (1980).

18
00:48:46,451 --> 00:48:52,451
The first live-action adaptations were European television productions,
mostly unlicensed, made in the 1970s and early 1990s.

19
00:51:29,032 --> 00:51:35,032
New Line Cinema produced the Lord of the Rings film trilogy (2001–2003) directed by
Jackson, and later returned to produce his Hobbit film trilogy (2012–2014).

20
00:54:11,612 --> 00:54:17,612
The New Line franchise has received a record 37 Academy Award
nominations, winning 17, and a record three special awards.

21
00:56:54,193 --> 00:57:00,193
To prevent New Line from losing the film adaptation rights, an
anime prequel film was put into production.

22
00:59:36,774 --> 00:59:42,774
Subtitled The War of the Rohirrim, it was released in 2024.

23
01:02:19,354 --> 01:02:25,354
After Middle-earth Enterprises was acquired by Embracer Group, a new
deal was struck with New Line to make two new films.

24
01:05:01,935 --> 01:05:07,935
The first was given the working title The Hunt for
Gollum and is scheduled for release in 2027.

25
01:07:44,516 --> 01:07:50,516
In 2017, Amazon Prime Video bought the right to make a
television series, separate from the New Line films.

26
01:10:27,096 --> 01:10:33,096
Titled The Lord of the Rings: The Rings of Power, the first
season was released in 2022 and the second in 2024.

27
01:13:09,677 --> 01:13:15,677
Three more seasons are planned.

28
01:15:52,258 --> 01:15:58,258
Some well-received fan films based on Tolkien's novels have been made; The Hunt
for Gollum and Born of Hope were both uploaded to YouTube in 2009.

29
01:18:34,838 --> 01:18:40,838
The film explores themes of satellite, disaster, white hole, rotation.

30
01:21:17,419 --> 01:21:23,419
The tone is thought_provoking.
